🔬 Methodological Justification & Source Suitability
1. Environmental Calculation Recognized Standards (EEA / DEFRA / IPCC)
Standard Selection: Rather than using generic estimates, Module 1 applies the European Environment Agency (EEA) / DEFRA 2025 standard factor of 2.68 kg $\text{CO}_2$ per Liter of diesel burned.
IPCC Consistency: This factor directly aligns with the IPCC Guidelines for National Greenhouse Gas Inventories (Tier 1 default emission factors for stationary and off-road transport engines), providing academic rigor and regulatory compliance.
Dorohusk Suitability: Heavy goods vehicles operating along the PL-UA corridor rely on standard Euro V and Euro VI diesel engines, supplemented by auxiliary Thermo King or Carrier diesel reefer units. The 2.68 kg $\text{CO}_2$/L factor accurately reflects the real-world carbon intensity of uncombusted idling diesel in Eastern European winter and summer operating conditions.
2. Operational & Queue Data (ZMPD / Polish Border Guard)
Standard Selection: Primary operational queue metrics ($T_{\text{delay}} = 42.50\text{ hours}$) are derived directly from the Association of International Road Transport Carriers in Poland (ZMPD) and Polish Border Guard daily telemetry logs.
Dorohusk Suitability: ZMPD provides real-time, empirical queue monitoring for the Dorohusk-Yagodin crossing. Using locally grounded data prevents overestimating potential time savings and ensures our baseline reflects actual border friction faced by 3PL dispatchers.
3. Carbon Quota Benchmarks (ICE EU ETS)
Standard Selection: Carbon pricing is anchored to the ICE Dec Carbon Futures Index (€80.00 / Tonne $\text{CO}_2$).
Dorohusk Suitability: As Dorohusk is an official entry gateway into the European Union, European freight carriers and importers are subject to EU Corporate Sustainability Due Diligence Directive (CSDDD) and CSRD Scope 3 reporting. Valuing avoided carbon at the EU ETS benchmark gives public authorities and corporate shippers an audit-ready financial measure of environmental benefits.
